-- =============================================================================
-- Clause-matrix analysis vs the column-wildcard idiom under constructor
-- shadowing: the GIVEN parameter `active` reuses the name of a nullary
-- constructor of its own column type. Per the pattern-matching spec, a bare
-- clause pattern that reuses its column's GIVEN name is the WILDCARD idiom —
-- the desugarer (L4.Parser.patAlwaysMatchesAs) skips the column entirely, so
-- clause 2 matches ALL Status values at runtime and the group is total:
-- `f suspended` is 2, not a NonExhaustivePatterns error.
--
-- The clause-matrix analysis must agree: re-resolving the bare `active`
-- pattern through the constructor-first pattern checker would mis-read row 2
-- as covering only the `active` constructor and warn that `suspended` is
-- missing — a false positive that the DMN leg would escalate to a D-PARTIAL
-- Blocking on a sound program. This golden pins the ABSENCE of any
-- PatternClausesMissing warning here (TypeCheck.patIsColumnWildcard mirrors
-- the desugarer), alongside the runtime results proving totality.
DECLARE Status IS ONE OF active, inactive, suspended

GIVEN active IS A Status
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE f inactive IS 1
DECIDE f active IS 2

-- Clause 2 is a wildcard: every non-`inactive` value takes it.
#EVAL f suspended -- 2, NOT a non-exhaustive-patterns error
#EVAL f active    -- 2
#EVAL f inactive  -- 1
